{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>You shall not steal.</b> It reverts to the plural form. Since in the Ten Commandments it was written לא תגנוב in the singular, here it is written in the plural, and He did not wish to make them equivalent, for they are distinct matters: there it speaks of theft of an item for which there is liability to the death penalty of the court, in the manner of לא תרצח and לא תנאף, whereas here it speaks of theft of money, for which one is liable to make restitution. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Bava Metzia: לא תגנבו — even ע\"מ למיקט, that is, the intention is not to keep the theft for himself but rather to cause distress to his fellow and provoke him; and the word למיקט is like להקניט, from the language of the verse (Job 10:1): ״נקטה נפשי בחיי״, (Ezekiel 2): ״ונקוטותם בפניכם״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory needed to interpret thus because it is a superfluous verse, for behold it is written לא תגזול, and anyone who steals is a robber, and if so, the prohibition of theft is already included in לא תגזול; and why was לא תגנבו written here? Even ע\"מ למיקט." "<b>And it says: You shall not deny falsely, and you shall not lie, and you shall not swear.</b> For if one steals, in the end he comes to swear falsely; in the end he profanes the Name, for one transgression drags in another. ולא תשבעו בשמי — and was it not already said לא תשא? One might think he is liable only for the distinctive Name — from where to include all the appellations? The verse states ולא תשבעו בשמי — any name that I have; thus did our Rabbis of blessed memory interpret." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ולא תשבעו בשמי — the great Name, as in (Numbers 6:27): ״ושמו את שמי״. And if one swears by it falsely, behold he profanes the Name, for he separates from it its force and root. This is what is meant by ״וחללת את שם אלהיך״. And thus the Ramban of blessed memory wrote, and this is his language by way of the truth: לא תשבעו בשמי לשקר — this is the distinctive Name, and ״וחללת את שם אלהיך״ — this is the name אלהים, from which all the appellations multiply. And there it was stated first: לא תשא את שם ה׳ אלהיך, and likewise ״לא ינקה ה׳ לשמו הגדול המיוחד״, and this is the meaning of ״וחללת את שם אלהיך״ — for when one swears by the distinctive Name falsely, he profanes the name of his Elohim; end quote. And thus you learn that all who swear by His Name profane the name of his Elohim, and one who bears the name of his Elohim — HaShem will not acquit him, and this is to indicate that all is bound and unified and unified, and included one within the other."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:58.479748047Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}